Negative control — the ADDRESSABILITY predicate's CLIENT-ID limb (AC-3).

Mutates `isAddressable` to drop its second conjunct: `isAuthenticated.value &&
!!clientId` becomes `isAuthenticated.value`. The predicate survives, every call
site still calls it, and it still refuses an unauthenticated session — it just
stops caring whether there is a client to address, so a session with no client
id is treated as addressable and requests go out at `clients/undefined/...`.
That is `parity.yaml` L1's shape: the check is present and does not check.

Declared AC — AC-3: "an unauthenticated session issues no list request at all"
(the request-capture log is EMPTY and `useMeta().isAvailable` is `false`;
asserting that an error surfaced does not discriminate).

Collateral RED, measured, and NOT this control's declared subject — both drive
their read-back through the same one predicate:
  - AC-11 — `remove` issues no request for a session with no client id
  - AC-13 — `setDefault` issues no request for a session with no client id

Those two have their own dedicated controls, which attack the PRECONDITION at
each call site rather than the predicate itself:
`client-address.auth-guard.must-fail.patch` (AC-11) and
`client-address.default-guard.must-fail.patch` (AC-13). One mutant per
capability, so a red localises: a red here means the predicate lost its client
limb; a red there means one call site stopped honouring it.

